  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1114 PM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nSunday through Friday:\n\nRidging remaining across Lake Michigan will keep the majority of\nsouthern Wisconsin dry on Sunday, with some potential for \nisolated showers with lingering moisture and WAA in the \nvicinity. Highs in the 60s are expected. Low pressure in the \nPlains deepens Sunday night as additional troughing propagates \neastward over the Rockies and interacts with the surface low in \nthe vicinity. This will lead to increasing southerly winds into \nMonday morning, leading to continued highs in the upper 60s. As \nlow pressure deepens throughout Monday, it will eject into the \nnorthern Plains, bringing increasing rain chances (50-80%) to \nsouthern Wisconsin within the WAA region of the low. Low \npressure continues to propagate northeastward into Minnesota by \nTuesday morning, keeping southern Wisconsin within the WAA \nregion and bringing continued rounds of rainfall (60-80% \nchances) throughout Monday night into Tuesday.\n\nUncertainty remains in the strength of low pressure, with the Euro\nindicating a stronger low and the GFS and Canadian indicating a\nstronger Northeast U.S. high preventing low pressure from\ncontinuing to propagate eastward and weakening its impacts.\nRegardless of exact model solution, precipitation will continue\nacross southern Wisconsin along the occluded front of this system\nthrough Tuesday into Tuesday night as the low propagates into\nwestern Ontario (40-60% chances precipitation through the period).\nA transition to the cold frontal feature of this system occurs \nWednesday, bringing an end to highs in the 60s by Thursday and a\nreturn to highs in the 50s. With this cold front in the vicinity\ninto Thursday, continued shower and storm potential may also \nlinger (20-35%).\n\nA cold high pressure system sliding southward through the Canadian\nPrairies will bring a return to northwesterly winds, dry\nconditions, and highs in the low 50s by Friday.\n\nMH",
